{"subjectId":"java","id":"string-memory-traps","title":"String & Memory Interview Traps","group":"JVM Internals & Memory","order":68,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A collection of classic “what does this print?” questions about strings, the pool, boxing and constant folding — each verified.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>== on strings</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String a = \"java\";\n        String b = \"java\";                 // same pooled literal\n        String c = new String(\"java\");     // a new heap object\n        String d = \"ja\" + \"va\";            // compile-time constant → folded to \"java\"\n        String part = \"ja\";\n        String e = part + \"va\";            // runtime concatenation → new object\n        final String fpart = \"ja\";\n        String f = fpart + \"va\";           // final + literal is a constant → folded\n\n        System.out.println(a == b);\n        System.out.println(a == c);\n        System.out.println(a == d);\n        System.out.println(a == e);\n        System.out.println(a == f);\n        System.out.println(a == e.intern());\n        System.out.println(a.equals(c));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\nfalse\ntrue\nfalse\ntrue\ntrue\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Pooled?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>\"java\"</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Every string literal is interned in the pool</td></tr><tr><td><code>new String(\"java\")</code></td><td>No</td><td><code>new</code> always creates a fresh heap object</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"ja\" + \"va\"</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Constant expression, folded by <code>javac</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>part + \"va\"</code> (non-final <code>part</code>)</td><td>No</td><td>Computed at run time (<code>StringConcatFactory</code> / <code>StringBuilder</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>fpart + \"va\"</code> (<code>final</code> with constant initialiser)</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>fpart</code> is a <em>constant variable</em>, so the whole expression is constant</td></tr><tr><td><code>s.intern()</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Returns the pooled instance, adding it if absent</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Rule: compare strings with <code>equals</code> (or <code>Objects.equals</code> for null-safety). <code>==</code> only tells you whether two references point at the same object.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How many objects does new String(\"hello\") create?</summary><div><p>Up to two: the literal <code>\"hello\"</code> is placed in the string pool when the class is loaded (if it isn't already there), and <code>new</code> creates one more <code>String</code> object on the heap. If the literal was already pooled, only one new object is created at run time.</p></div></details>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Integer cache and boxing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Integer a = 127, b = 127;\n        Integer c = 128, d = 128;\n        System.out.println(a == b);          // cached: -128..127\n        System.out.println(c == d);          // two different objects\n        System.out.println(c.equals(d));\n\n        Long big = 127L;\n        System.out.println(big.equals(127));  // Integer vs Long → false\n        System.out.println(big == 127);       // unboxed numeric comparison → true\n\n        Integer missing = null;\n        try {\n            int n = missing;                  // auto-unboxing null\n            System.out.println(n);\n        } catch (NullPointerException ex) {\n            System.out.println(\"NPE on unboxing\");\n        }\n\n        boolean flag = true;\n        try {\n            int r = flag ? missing : 0;       // ternary unboxes `missing`\n            System.out.println(r);\n        } catch (NullPointerException ex) {\n            System.out.println(\"NPE in ternary\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\nfalse\ntrue\nfalse\ntrue\nNPE on unboxing\nNPE in ternary</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The cache covers <code>Integer</code>, <code>Short</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Byte</code> in −128…127, <code>Character</code> up to 127 and both <code>Boolean</code> values. The upper bound for <code>Integer</code> can be raised with <code>-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax</code>, so <code>==</code> on boxed numbers is never reliable.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Concatenation and char arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"a\" + 1 + 2);      // left to right: (\"a\" + 1) + 2\n        System.out.println(1 + 2 + \"a\");      // (1 + 2) + \"a\"\n        System.out.println('a' + 'b');        // char + char → int\n        System.out.println(\"\" + 'a' + 'b');\n        System.out.println((char) ('a' + 1));\n        System.out.println(Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE));   // overflow: no positive counterpart\n        System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a12\n3a\n195\nab\nb\n-2147483648\n0.30000000000000004</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>String + in a loop</h3>\n<p>Each <code>s += x</code> creates a brand-new string and copies everything so far — O(n²) for n appends. Inside loops use a <code>StringBuilder</code> (single-expression concatenations are fine: the compiler optimises them).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {\n            sb.append(i).append(',');\n        }\n        sb.setLength(sb.length() - 1);            // drop the trailing comma\n        System.out.println(sb);\n\n        System.out.println(String.join(\",\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));   // often simplest\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0,1,2,3,4\na,b,c</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Other traps</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scissors\"></i><code>substring</code> copies</h3><p>Before Java 7u6, <code>substring</code> shared the parent's <code>char[]</code> — a tiny substring could pin a huge string in memory. Now it copies, so no leak (but O(k) cost).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Passwords in <code>char[]</code></h3><p>A <code>String</code> is immutable and may be pooled; it lingers in memory until GC and can show up in heap dumps. A <code>char[]</code> can be wiped with <code>Arrays.fill(pw, '\\0')</code> right after use — that's why <code>Console.readPassword()</code> returns <code>char[]</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-zip\"></i>Compact strings (9+)</h3><p>Strings that contain only Latin-1 characters store one byte per char instead of two (<code>byte[]</code> + a coder flag).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-square\"></i>Strings are immutable</h3><p><code>s.toUpperCase()</code> returns a new string; calling it without assigning the result does nothing.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = \"hello\";\n        s.toUpperCase();                   // result discarded\n        System.out.println(s);\n        s = s.toUpperCase();\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hello\nHELLO</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch\"></i>switch on strings and null</h3>\n<p>A <code>switch</code> on a <code>String</code> compiles to a switch on <code>hashCode()</code> followed by <code>equals()</code>. A <code>null</code> selector throws <code>NullPointerException</code> — unless (Java 21) you add <code>case null</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static String classic(String cmd) {\n        switch (cmd) {\n            case \"start\": return \"starting\";\n            case \"stop\":  return \"stopping\";\n            default:      return \"unknown\";\n        }\n    }\n\n    static String modern(String cmd) {\n        return switch (cmd) {\n            case null -&gt; \"no command\";            // Java 21\n            case \"start\" -&gt; \"starting\";\n            default -&gt; \"unknown\";\n        };\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(classic(\"stop\"));\n        try {\n            classic(null);\n        } catch (NullPointerException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"NPE from classic switch\");\n        }\n        System.out.println(modern(null));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>stopping\nNPE from classic switch\nno command</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is String immutable in Java?</summary><div><p>(1) The string pool is only safe if pooled strings can't change. (2) Security: class names, file paths and URLs passed to APIs can't be altered after a check. (3) Thread safety for free. (4) The hash code can be cached, which makes strings fast <code>HashMap</code> keys.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does intern() do, and should you use it?</summary><div><p>It returns the canonical pooled copy of the string, adding it to the pool if needed, so equal interned strings are <code>==</code>. It can save memory for many duplicate strings, but the pool lookup has a cost — modern G1 also offers <code>-XX:+UseStringDeduplication</code>, which deduplicates backing arrays automatically.</p></div></details></section>"}